Negative control — the COUNTRY LOCK on an existing address (AC-21, fix 8d,
ruling R8d, parity rows W11/L4).

Forces `lockCountry` to a constant `false`, so the country control's DISABLE
rule is never emitted and the field stays editable however the brand is
configured. The `CLIENT_ALLOW_ADDRESS_UPDATE` key is still fetched, still
reaches the form through `config`, and the uischema still builds — the value is
simply never consulted, which is the pre-conversion state the story fixed.

Declared AC — AC-21: "the country field is locked on edit when the brand
forbids address updates" — with `CLIENT_ALLOW_ADDRESS_UPDATE === false` and an
EXISTING address id, the country control carries the disabled/read-only rule;
with no id (create), under the very same forbidding config, it does not.

No collateral measured. Note which half of AC-21 can move: the CREATE half
asserts the rule is ABSENT, and it is absent under this mutant too, so it stays
GREEN — only the EDIT half flips. That asymmetry is the discriminator: an
always-off lock is indistinguishable from a correct one on the create path, and
AC-21 is written with both halves precisely so a constant cannot pass.

Unrelated: AC-20's standing failure (finding F6, escalated per
`review-notes.md` §7.6) is red with or without this mutant — no
`config/brand/values` request is issued at all. The KEY still reaches the form,
which is what this control's AC proves.

